# CODEOWNERS — GitHub Code Ownership
# ====================================
# This file maps repository paths to the GitHub users or teams responsible
# for reviewing pull requests that touch those paths. GitHub automatically
# requests reviews from the matching owner(s) when a PR is opened.
#
# HOW THIS FILE WAS GENERATED
# ----------------------------
# Initial owners were derived from `git log -1 --format="%ae" -- <dir>/`
# (the last person who committed to each top-level directory) and then
# mapped to GitHub usernames. Update entries as ownership changes — git
# history is a starting point, not a permanent source of truth.
#
# FORMAT
# ------
# <path pattern>    @owner1 @owner2 ...
#
# Patterns are matched top-to-bottom; the LAST matching rule wins.
# A catch-all `*` at the top ensures nothing is ever unowned.
# More specific rules below override it for their paths.
#
# TEAMS vs INDIVIDUALS
# ---------------------
# Prefer team handles (@IBM/team-name) over individuals where possible so
# that ownership survives people changing roles or leaving the project.
# Individual handles are used here as a bootstrap — migrate to teams over time.
#
# REFERENCE
# ----------
# https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners

# ── Catch-all fallback ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any path not matched by a more specific rule below is owned by the core team.
# This ensures every file always has at least one owner.
*                               @IBM/z-ansible-collections

# ── Workflow and repository configuration ─────────────────────────────────────
# Changes to CI, lint config, and repo metadata are reviewed by the designated
# infrastructure maintainer. Only one reviewer is assigned to avoid spamming
# the entire team for workflow/config changes.
